22:27:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You’re absolutely right that the colors we see in the physical world come from the way light interacts with materials. A red apple, for example, looks red because when white light (like sunlight) hits it, the apple’s surface absorbs most of the other wavelengths and reflects primarily the red wavelengths into our eyes. That’s called **subtractive color**—the object “subtracts” certain colors from the light. A computer or TV screen works on a completely different principle: it doesn’t reflect ambient light; it **emits its own light**. This is known as **additive color**. Here’s the basic idea: --- ### Pixels and subpixels If you look very closely at a screen (a magnifying glass helps), you’ll see that the whole image is made of millions of tiny dots called **pixels**. Each pixel is actually composed of three even tinier light sources: one **red**, one **green**, and one **blue** (RGB). These are often just tiny colored filters over a white backlight (in LCDs) or individual light-emitting diodes (in OLEDs). ### Mixing light Because our eyes have three types of color-sensitive cone cells that roughly correspond to red, green, and blue light, we can create the perception of almost any color by mixing those three primary light colors in different proportions. - To make **yellow**, the screen turns on the red and green subpixels at full brightness, but leaves blue off. (Red + Green light makes yellow.) - To make **white**, it turns on all three at full brightness. - To make **black**, it simply turns all three off (or blocks the backlight entirely in an LCD). By varying the **intensity** of each subpixel from 0% to 100% (usually with 256 steps per color, giving 16.7 million combinations), the screen can simulate a huge gamut of colors. --- ### Why it’s “additive” In the physical world, mixing paints or inks is *subtractive* because each pigment absorbs some light. Start with white paper, add cyan, magenta, and yellow inks, and you subtract more and more light, eventually getting black. On a screen, we start with a black background (no light), and we **add** red, green, and blue light together to build up any color. Add them all at full intensity and you get white. That’s why it’s called additive color. --- ### Recap of the difference | Physical Objects (Reflective) | Screens (Emissive) | |-------------------------------|---------------------| | Rely on external light source | Generate their own light | | Color is what’s *not* absorbed | Color is what light is *emitted* | | Primary colors for mixing: cyan, magenta, yellow (subtractive) | Primary colors: red, green, blue (additive) | | Mixing all paints gives black | Mixing all lights gives white | So while your original mental model is correct for paint, flowers, and everything around you, a screen is more like a field of tiny, precisely controlled colored light bulbs that blend together in your eye to create the illusion of a full-color picture.
