22:27:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great observation, and you’re right—many screens do reflect light, sometimes acting like a dark mirror. However, that reflected light is not part of the image the screen is supposed to show. It’s more like noise that gets mixed in, potentially making the picture harder to see. Let’s break it down: ### The image itself is still purely additive The colors you see in the intended picture (the movie, the webpage, the game) are created entirely by the screen’s own red, green, and blue subpixels turning on at different intensities. That part hasn’t changed—it’s still additive, emitted light. ### The screen’s surface is a separate thing But the screen isn’t just a pure light source floating in darkness; it has a physical surface—usually glass or a plastic coating. Just like a window or a still lake, that surface can reflect ambient light (sunlight, a lamp, your face) into your eyes. This reflection is subtractive in nature: the ambient light bounces off the screen, just like it would off any object. ### Two kinds of light hitting your eye When you look at a screen in a bright room, two things are reaching your eye: 1. **The emitted image light** (the intended picture). 2. **Reflected ambient light** (the glare and reflections of the room). Your eye adds them together. The reflected glare doesn’t change the fact that the screen *creates* its colors by emitting light; it just overlays an extra, unwanted layer of light on top. That’s why a bright room makes a screen look washed out—the darkest black you can see is no longer the screen’s switched-off pixel; it’s the brightness of the reflected room lights. ### To be precise - The *mechanism* by which the screen makes color is still emission (additive RGB), not reflection. - The *screen as a physical object* does reflect light, but that reflection is a separate optical phenomenon that interferes with, rather than produces, the image. So while a screen is indeed a reflective surface in the general physical sense, its job as a display device is to overwhelm or outshine those reflections with its own emitted light. That’s why modern screens have anti-glare coatings or high brightness—to reduce the visibility of those reflections so you see the additive picture, not the mirror. In short: reflections are a real-world annoyance, but they’re not part of the color-making mechanism. The picture on the screen is still painted with tiny light bulbs, not by reflecting sunlight like a painted canvas.
